r  aid to be released and obviously western  governments don't want to look like they  are supporting the taliban how is this  now being discussed in in western  capitals yeah my organization the  international crisis group was one of  the sort of earliest to come out  publicly last fall and say look um this  is not going to work uh you know there  are a lot of hungry people in  afghanistan and so it's probably time to  start thinking about unfreezing the  frozen assets  about lifting some of the  western economic restrictions especially  easing the effects of sanctions  and to their credit actually uh western  actors especially the united states have  been taking steps in that direction i  mean this spring we saw something called  general license 20 from the u.s treasury  department that's actually the most  sweeping set of exemptions from u.s  sanctions anywhere in the world so there  have been the this kind of you know  effort to  to ease the blow a little bit but it's  still very much a work in progress those  assets remain frozen as you mentioned at  the top of the show  and uh those negotiations are continuing  between uh the united states and the  taliban about the future of central  banking which sounds like a boring topic  but it's just vital to how afghans eat  there needs to be cash liquidity on the  streets so that the import deals can get  done so that you know daily bread can  reach uh afghan bakeries  so well let's talk about what that looks  like on the streets of kabul musca you  alluded to the economic crisis there  what does a regular day look like at the  moment  the regular day it um  it's disappointing it's painful and it's  heartbreaking um those those are all  that i can say but um  behind every  bread bakery that you see there are 20  to 20 25 30 women just sitting there  waiting for someone to give them a piece  of bread that's what's happening  children are out on the streets begging  all day long  most of these women were the only  breadwinners of their family since their  husbands or brothers or fathers have  been killed or died or  all  during these 20 years of war so they  were the main  breadwinners and now  a daily  normal day in kabul or in afghanistan  overall looks like  this sad tragedy where women are out  with their hands out begging for 10 apps  20 amps a piece of bread a little bit of  food so they could go back home and feed  their children that's what it looks like  well we keep talking about food security  and i recall  last winter we were talking very much  about the possibility of a famine in  afghanistan going into the lean season  it does seem that somehow that was  staved off graham how how did that  actually happen and obviously there's  there's winter coming again this year  but what is the situation like at the  moment  i mean it was staved off just barely  uh the united nations says that upwards  of 20 000 people in the central  highlands are now in what they call  famine-like conditions that's sort of  the very worst kind of starvation-like  category  and there's i think there's a real fear  that that could spread  as we get towards winter afghanistan of  course produces a lot of its own food  in the harvest seasons and then when you  get towards winter it depends a lot more  on imports those imports are now more  expensive globally partly because of the  war in ukraine and partly because of the  paralysis in the banking sector it's  very hard for food importers to bring  food into the country like they did  before and some of the the blockages uh  to to trade  are resulting in higher prices for for  ordinary afghans as you noted um you  know just terrible inflation not just in  the price of food the price of fuel the  prices of medicine  and so uh there really does have to be  some greater action  um in terms of uh reaching some kind of  agreement between the west and the  taliban on how economic revival is going  to be achieved moscow correct me if i'm  wrong i understand