ral villages  people are are putting their lives back  together they're picking up the pieces  literally  um some of the the biggest threats to  civilians in those areas are when  children pick up the explosive remnants  of war they're they're foraging for  scrap metal or they're playing with the  shiny bits of metal and something  explodes and so far too many children  unfortunately uh are dying uh in the  remnants of this terrible conflict  musket you're sitting in kabul do you  feel like there's still a huge huge  difference between how people feel in  the capital versus in the rural areas  particularly now that the security  situation has changed  honestly i think right now everyone is  starving be it the capital be it the  rural areas everyone's focus is to have  their tummies filled uh food on their  table um security  i mean  what i mean  it does not feel secure so what's the  point of this kind of a security okay  yes we're not having explosions the way  we used to but they are they were the  ones who were doing the explosions  themselves so i mean that is not that  big of a  good news that oh explosions or the  security has uh  gotten better um but right now as  a whole the whole nation right now is  starving and that's their biggest  biggest issue right now at hand um  i mean if you really think about it what  good does this security bring if they're  all starving and are dealing with  depression or anxiety or  a hundred other issues that they face on  daily basis well amid all the criticism  that the taliban's received they've said  that their big focus has been on  security and graham as you alluded to  there they are still fighting a number  of groups including isil what do you  think their focus is now are they being  distracted by by military operations are  they wanting to focus on the economic  situation  the taliban are in the middle of  consolidating control both militarily  and economically  um yes as you say uh they are still  struggling against