 that there is food to  be bought in the market so it is  available it's just that people don't  have the money to buy it and i know  there have been some issues about  salaries being paid so can you give us  an idea of just how big that gap is  between the price of food and what  people can actually afford  um well the price of food has got  has gone really high  um of course and but the thing is that  currently half of the  the people or women that we're working  since i come and i am  basically representing the women right  now but the women used to work and now  they have been fired from their jobs so  they're not really getting salaries and  if they were the breadwinners it's  really hard uh so that gap is like  really really really big um  and so um i mean i really don't know how  this is gonna this is gonna change um  because um also the donations that so  far have come in um to help uh the  people who are dealing with uh with such  issues it has only made afghanistan look  like a charity case and that's not what  we want we want something stable we want  something  for everyone to have their rights to  right to work right education right to  just equal rights for men and women um  currently most men are not being paid  most of their salaries are not given so  i mean  people literally don't have money to go  and buy  and  enough for for their families so they're  dealing with quite a hard time right now  some of the most extreme needs that  we've been seeing are in rural areas as  well so many people though outside kabul  just wanted the fighting to stop so now  we're seeing this relative peace at the  same time as economic challenges and  mounting graham how are people outside  kabul viewing the taliban now given this  kind of dichotomous situation they're in  well you know  as you mentioned  shutting millions of girls out of high  schools  really hasn't helped the taliban's  reputation  i think the discovery of al qaeda leader  ayman al-zawahiri living in the heart of  kabul also damaged the taliban's  reputation globally  and so uh it gets harder under those  conditions um for the taliban to reach  any kind of  um agreement about how to get beyond  just sort of short-term emergency relief  which is neither you know sustainable  nor sufficient to deal with the tens of  millions of afghans who are now in the  brink of starvation  you know what the world bank has said is  that we need to start investing in  development that for example  there is less area under effective  irrigation today than there was in the  1970s  it's a primarily agricultural economy so  you know there needs to be help with  getting water to crops uh but you know  who is going to come in and build a  large-scale irrigation system  uh under a taliban regime that is not  recognized by the international  community and  um you know with the sort of political  distance growing and so it's  um it really is a a difficult situation  graham i i really want to focus on how  people in rural areas particularly  feeling as well because i know you've  traveled around the country i mean in  the last few months or so and i'm  curious about how people who were really  just wanting the fighting to stop how  they're feeling now that they're faced  with the taliban regime even though  there is a now relative security  yeah i just  finished working with colleagues on a  report about the security situation  based on interviews in 13 out of the 34  provinces  a lot of people in rural areas are  benefiting from  the end of the war essentially there are  two small insurgencies remaining one  against remnants of the former republic  in the north and one against the local  version of the islamic state in the east  but these are really small scale uh  conflicts and you know this had been the  deadliest war on the planet earth we had  thirty thousand to forty thousand people  uh dying annually in the conflict  hundreds of thousands displaced and so  yeah in a lot of the the rural villages  people are are putting their lives back  together they're picking up the pieces  literally  um some of the the biggest threats to  civilians in those areas are when  children pick up the explosive remnants  of war they're they're foraging for  scrap metal or they're playing with the  shiny bits of metal and something  explodes and so far too many children  unfortunately uh are dying uh in the  remnants of this terrible conflict  musket you're sitting in kabul do you  feel like there's still a huge huge  difference between how people feel in  the capital versus in the rural areas  particularly now that the security  situation has changed  honestly i think right now everyone is  starving be it the capital be it the  rural areas everyone's focus is to have  their tummies filled uh food on their  table um security  i mean  what i mean  it does not feel secure so what's the  point of this kind of a security okay  yes we're not having explosions the way  we used to but they are they were the  ones who were doing the explosions  themselves so i mean that is not that  big of a  good news that oh explosions or the  security has uh  gotten better um but right now as  a whole the whole nation right now is  starving and that's their biggest  biggest issue right now at hand um  i mean if you really think about it what  good does this security bring if they're  all starving and are dealing with  depression or anxiety or  a hundred other issues that they face on  daily basis well amid all the criticism  that the taliban's received they've said  that their big focus has been on  security and graham as you alluded to  there they are still fighting a number  of groups including isil what do you  think their focus is now are they being  distracted by by military operations are  they wanting to focus on the economic  situation  the taliban are in the middle of  consolidating control both militarily  and economically  um yes as you say uh they are still  struggling against uh  some you could call them insurgent  groups um they are actually gaining the  upper hand against  these uh pockets of resistance but they  they do continue  uh  economically uh  they have uh just  dismantled some of the checkpoints um  you know when you drive  around the highways uh  some of the places where the police used  to shake down truck drivers those places  simply don't exist anymore  and some very uh careful satellite-based  work examining flows through customs  houses has shown that the taliban have  cleaned up more than a billion dollars a  year of corruption that occurred during  the last  government and so there is now today a  greater percentage of the money being  handed over at customs points is going  to the central treasury so that helps  the taliban to uh mitigate some of the  the economic uh damage but we're talking  about an economy that has shrunk  considerably uh and so overall uh even  though the taliban are uh consolidating  some of their their power economically  and militarily  yeah it is just a a a  situation of lower demand and so um it's  very difficult for them to to navigate  that you were speaking about them  consolidating power there are still  splits within the taliban and obviously  you alluded also to the previous  government  i wonder whether there have been revenge  killings that one of the biggest  promises that they made in august was  that there would be an amnesty for  people from the previous government that  these revenge killings wouldn't take  place that there would be freedom of the  media as well musker can you give us a  sense of the sentiment there you're  obviously  comfortable speaking to us openly now  what does that say about the situation  maybe i'm just a little brave and i like  taking risks that's why i am staying the  way or speaking rather the way that i am  but media is extremely controlled um  they're not allowed to either it's very  restricted for them they cannot  say everything on the news it's  